      "id": "descriptive_only",
      "statement": "Every number describes the rows in this file and nothing else. No statistic here is an estimate for a wider population, a test or a prediction."
    },
    {
      "id": "header_and_shape",
      "statement": "The first non-blank row is the header. Every row must have as many fields as the header; a file where some do not is rejected rather than padded or trimmed. Blank lines are skipped and counted."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_values",
      "statement": "A cell is missing if, after trimming spaces, it is empty or exactly equal (case-sensitive) to one of the missing-value markers. Markers can be wrong for your data: 'NA' is also the country code for Namibia, so edit the list when needed."
    },
    {
      "id": "type_rules",
      "statement": "Numbers are plain decimal or scientific notation (no thousands separators). Dates are ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD, optional time) and must exist on the calendar. 01/02/2024-style dates are ambiguous and stay text. Numbers with leading zeros stay text unless you override the column. A column with any other value is categorical; nothing is converted silently."
    },
    {
      "id": "overrides_and_invalid",
      "statement": "Overriding a column's type never changes the data. Values that do not fit the chosen type are counted as invalid, shown, and left out of the statistics."
    },
    {
      "id": "duplicates",
      "statement": "A duplicate row is identical, character for character, to an earlier row (so ' UK' and 'UK' differ, and so do '1' and '1.0'). Duplicates are counted, not removed."
    },
    {
      "id": "statistics_definitions",
      "statement": "Mean; sample standard deviation with n - 1; quartiles by linear interpolation (Hyndman-Fan type 7, as in NumPy and spreadsheets' QUARTILE.INC, which can differ from the 'median of each half' method for small samples). Percentages are of all rows. Computed in double precision from the parsed values, so 0.1 is stored as the nearest binary fraction; agreement with exact arithmetic is to about 1e-15 relative, not exact."
    }
